WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:07.910
Hi, this is Bizhan Tong and I’m the writer,
producer and self-funder of THE ESCORT, a

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:11.750
feature chronicling gender perception within
society by way of the industry.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:15.860
Now today I’m here to talk to you about
5 problems I faced when making THE ESCORT,

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.289
the trials and tribulations we encountered,
what it would mean for production and how

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:20.400
we overcame them.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.449
But before I do so let’s take a brief moment
to recap the journey and explain how we got

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:26.449
to this point.

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:31.400
Now for those of you watching I’ an avid
fan of film, molded by it, living it, breathing

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.890
it, dependent on it.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.450
And after writing my first feature screenplay
at the age of 12 I knew that was what I wanted

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.650
to do with my life.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.420
I’ve spent almost everyday since writing
or tinkering with scripts.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.150
I made my first proper short in 2008 for the
short film called TICKET about toxic masculinity

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.830
and earned my first producing credit that
same year with FIX, a feature starring Olivia

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:50.830
Wilde.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:56.350
Then after I continued to rise as later projects
and honed my craft to the course at Raindance

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:01.100
and volunteered to shoot corporate videos
for free in order to try out different styles

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.150
with someone I know production designer Charles
McCarry on TV series I was developing.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.920
I finally I formed a production company Phoenix
Waters Productions in 2015 in order to bring

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.149
my stories to life tell of different genres
amassed over a decade of all high-concepts

00:01:16.149 --> 00:01:21.050
dealing with personal and social themes and
the first feature I wanted to make was THE

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.600
ESCORT.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:29.520
Now I had no funding in place but I knew this
story was special and the right feature debut.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.240
So I set money aside from my day job to fund
the film.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.369
I spent several months interviewing current
and former workers to lend credence to the

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:38.369
script.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:42.369
It was important to me that their views be
echoed in the particular character’s words

00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:48.189
rather than my assumptions, wrote the screenplay
with budget constraints in mind, two characters,

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:51.723
one location and spoke with an accomplished
female director about helming the project

00:01:51.723 --> 00:01:55.460
but exploring the escort industry wasn’t
of interest to her.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.749
And so I made the decision that this would
be my feature directorial debut as well.

00:02:00.749 --> 00:02:05.619
Cut to almost three years later and The Escort
is about the premiere in New York at the Kew

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:06.990
Gardens Festival of Cinema.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.900
Reaction from those who’ve seen it has been
immensely positive.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.890
Distributor are already making offers.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:18.140
The company now has exceptional board of directors
and I’m busy prepping my next feature NIGHT

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.140
RIDE.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.850
The first few years weren’t easy and so
without further ado here are five problems

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.090
I faced when filming THE ESCORT.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.090
1.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.090
Timing

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.090
A nine day shoot.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.740
Actor Kevin Lesley plays the client Eric in
the movie and does a phenomenal job as well.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:38.740
Kevin had other film commitments at the time
which meant we only had 9 days to shoot a

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.670
feature because on day 10 he would have to
shave his head for a role.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.700
And knowing THE ESCORT takes place in real
time there would have been glaring continuity

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.970
errors and I didn’t want to slap him with
a Fantastic Four wig.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.660
Plus I didn’t have the budget for one.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:59.620
Now we had one month between casting Olivia
Moore as the title character and shooting

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.970
the film as it was still finalizing the crew
and searching for the right location to keep

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:04.980
production cost low.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.680
So I went to the studio room in canary wolf
where the actors and I would work on refining

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.400
the characters, their world and the execution
of their dialogue.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.930
I had a very specific approach in mind.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:20.350
The actors continued to rehearse themselves
in even the studio or on Skype and I went

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.220
for the script listing every shot I wanted.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.040
Please note I could only afford one camera.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.590
So for a lot of retakes it was important not
to keep the action static.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.460
That way I could keep production efficient
and ensure them we were moving at pace.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.400
Doing the shoot the camera op, DP and I would
hold a meeting every morning on the train

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.720
to discuss the shots we needed while in the
evening the actors would rehearse while I

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.090
would sleep at 2:00 a.m. the next night and
wake up at 3:30 for the following day’s

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.090
shoot.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:48.090
And kudos to the cast.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.820
There was one scene in the kitchen where it
was important to accomplish it in one take

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:58.070
which was a challenge for all in the technicolor
climate but especially for them as was a lot

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.320
of dialogue as they couldn’t afford to flub
a line.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:07.100
Everyone was passionate, everyone was involved
and on the last day we shot for 17 hours straight

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.940
in order to complete the film culminating
in shoot the bedroom scene which was the trickiest

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.950
and more emotional one to do.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.110
Fortunately morale was in tact which is the
key to success in any production.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:20.110
2.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.180
Noisy Neighbors!

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.719
A war zone next store.

00:04:22.719 --> 00:04:26.960
If I could make any change to THE ESCORT is
would be shoot at another location not because

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.460
of the location itself which I am grateful
to have been offered but because of the property

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.800
next door which sounded like a Michael Bay
blockbuster.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.490
Prior to the shoot I had been advised that
building works would be completed prior to

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:38.889
when principal photography began.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:43.759
Alas when we arrived on set we found this
wasn’t the case and had to battle our way

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:47.810
through the sound of power drills, saws and
heavy machinery for the duration of the shoot.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.360
While the builders were claiming we were shooting
a film, I never even told them the title.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.520
Ultimately there was a lot of negotiating
involved.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.919
Ultimately there was a lot of negotiation
involved and I would frequently visit the

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:01.280
house to arrange to arrange time slot of silence
or ask for a specific amount of time to get

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.280
a take right.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.330
But we also used that time to stay productive.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:09.229
When it was noisy we would shoot B-roll footage
or take lunch so we could shoot when they

00:05:09.229 --> 00:05:10.229
were on break.

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:13.999
And during an off-screen monologue when the
camera needed to focus on the listener’s

00:05:13.999 --> 00:05:18.499
reaction we decided to shoot the scene and
weave in the monologue in post.

00:05:18.499 --> 00:05:20.380
Now not everything was sorted this way.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.639
The bathroom scene which was shot perfectly
had to sound butchered.

00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:27.560
But thanks to ADR work by Phil Clemens and
a positive relationship with the builders

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.169
next door we were able to get through it and
the final tract is a testament to this and

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:35.610
it doesn’t look or sound like we faced these
problems.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:36.830
3.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.020
A change of DP

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.319
While the director has a vision.

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:45.319
The director of photography is operative to
the execution of that vision.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:49.830
And so the look, start and feel of the film
had been discussed well in advance as a DP

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.310
perceives it from a technical standpoint.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:56.370
Their style (much like the directors) permeating
each production.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:03.259
When the original DP needed to leave early
for personal reasons, this threatened to derail

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:04.259
the plan we had in place.

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:09.289
I was conscious that this would distract the
cast and crew as I needed them focused and

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:13.569
giving their all, particularly the actors
who had some challenging scenes to perform.

00:06:13.569 --> 00:06:17.830
So we agreed to keep this news from them after
the replacement was found with the DP continuing

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:19.490
until the end of the week.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:23.320
During each break from filming I would leave
the house in an effort to find someone on

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:29.259
such short notice and fortunately the DP introduced
me to someone (Beau Richard James) a steadicam

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:34.080
operator who agreed to step into the DP role
in order to keep production on track.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.849
The other problem was style as their visual
angles would be different than each other.

00:06:38.849 --> 00:06:42.840
Fortunately my intent had always been that
every scene would be shot in a subtly different

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.110
way to effect the story and those characters
in that moment.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.599
So how can that style be different from what
I was trying to do in the context of what

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:53.610
I was trying to do as long as it wasn’t
radically and Beau worked tirelessly along

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:59.050
with other camera operators often putting
in extra days to get the look and shot.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:03.030
We had this transitional period as we moved
from one DP to the next and because of his

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:07.180
production stayed on track and we crafted
some of the finest moments in the film from

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.100
the dance scene to the bedroom with our new
addition to the team.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:11.100
4.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.819
The British Weather

00:07:12.819 --> 00:07:17.069
Freakish wind and rain during a supposed sunny
out door shoot.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:20.990
THE ESCORT takes place in real time and it
was important that it set during the day since

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.860
nighttime shady activities tend to be synonamous
with each other.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.210
And I wanted to underline the activities that
go on in quiet, pleasant neighborhoods on

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.020
seemingly bright days.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.629
We had set one morning to set all the outdoor
scenes based on the schedule we had in place

00:07:34.629 --> 00:07:35.629
and the weather forecast.

00:07:35.629 --> 00:07:39.990
But unfortunately one of the freakiest moments
on the shoot, that morning was met with high

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.059
winds and light rain.

00:07:42.059 --> 00:07:44.879
That mean Kevin’s clothes were flapping
about in the wind and they had to almost shout

00:07:44.879 --> 00:07:46.680
their lines to be heard.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:50.330
The equipment had all been moved outside of
the shoot so prepping a different scene and

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:52.360
moving inside would have taken at least an
hour.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:56.830
And I knew based on the schedule shooting
in on a different day would have sacrificed

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:00.860
time on more technical and more technical
and emotionally challenging scenes.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.449
This is why I pose a question to you, what
would you do?

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:09.370
In our case Beau waited until mini hurricane
outside until there was a brief moment when

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:12.490
the winds and rain subsided and we were able
to shoot the scene.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.690
But we were extraordinarily fortunate to get
it.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.729
So I’d been keen to hear your thoughts.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:17.729
5.

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:19.119
No Backing Up The Backups

00:08:19.119 --> 00:08:20.749
Loosing the Climax of Act One

00:08:20.749 --> 00:08:26.150
If there is one piece of advice that I would
give anyone is to have a good day director.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:30.119
It was the biggest shock during production
that we lost the climax of Act 1.

00:08:30.119 --> 00:08:33.751
Not just the original but the back up leading
to subsequently forming multiple backups for

00:08:33.751 --> 00:08:35.760
the rest of production.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.099
One take remained in tact but it only showed
on of the actors and we knew we had no choice

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:44.810
but to reshoot say for a brief flirtation
of having it done anime style but it was something

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:45.810
we avoided.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:50.300
We prepped to shoot five months later when
Kevin had grown his hair but we no longer

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.769
had access to location and had to shoot at
Liverpool Street.

00:08:53.769 --> 00:08:56.710
Now this poses it’s own challenges because
it’s clear we’re shooting at a different

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:57.710
location.

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:00.970
And considering that we have the escort’s
apartment, this would be a problem.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:04.870
Now we looked at various options, maybe we’d
have a blow-out in the background and shoot

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:05.870
it there.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.110
But what we ultimately decided to do was to
have the props on the set and most importantly

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:15.360
the sofa since that is where the climax moment
takes place, bring that over to the set and

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.300
film in a very tight manner that would focus
on the characters’ faces.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:24.420
This actually worked ideally for us because
looking at the scene and what was occurring

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.079
there was an argument and we were looking
to break them apart.

00:09:27.079 --> 00:09:34.740
So from a visual standpoint we thought why
not just have them not share the scene?

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.250
So two days before we were about to shoot
the film, I had a personal issue and my Mom

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:45.000
was diagnosed with Cancer but this was important
that we keep it quiet from the cast and crew

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.500
again because I need them to focus and give
it their all.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.500
It was an emotional scene.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.690
It was an important scene to do.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:57.570
And so I worked with them, I kept these personal
things private and we were able to shoot the

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:58.570
scene.

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:03.810
We worked afterwards through editing, it enabled
us to utilize the scene that we had (that

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:08.820
one take) that we had in the location with
what we created afterwards.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:12.449
Fortunately it meshed well and it worked well
and I’m really proud and happy with what

00:10:12.449 --> 00:10:14.170
we’ve created.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:20.111
So those are the 5 problems I faced making
THE ESCORT and I hope you never have to face

00:10:20.111 --> 00:10:24.130
those during your production and ultimately
thank you very much for your time.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:26.670
I hope this was an educational one.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.230
And I wish you the best of luck with your
films.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:29.570
Thanks!

